\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\title{#{\Theta(\lg n)}: logarithmic (unimaginably large inputs)}

\p{We say something is \em{logarithmic} if it is proportional to #{\log n} for inputs of size #{n}.}

\transclude{00L4}  % lg notation

\p{If we have an algorithm that takes #{\Theta(\lg n)} time, we could use it to process inputs of size #{2^{10^8}} in one second, which is unimaginably large.}

\p{Examples of things that are #{\Theta(\lg n)}:

\ul{
  \li{Binary search}
  \li{The height of a balanced binary tree with #{n} nodes}
  \li{The number of bits needed to represent #{n}}
}
}

\transclude{00L9}   % base doesn't matter
\transclude{00L8}   % lg n is o(n^x)
\transclude{00LA}   % Remark re: 1 vs lg n vs n
